<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Rafael Mora Porras</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a>–<a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Rafael Mora Porras was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> merchant and president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> from 1849 to 1859. He led the Central American coalition that defeated the American filibuster <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> in 1856 and 1857, and is honoured as a national hero. Overthrown in 1859, he was executed after a failed attempt to return to power in 1860.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-rafael-mora-porras.jpg" alt="Juan Rafael Mora Porras" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aquiles Bigot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Rafael_Mora_Porras.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juan Rafael Mora, known as Don Juanito, was born on 8 February 1814 to Camilo Mora Alvarado and Ana Benita Porras Ulloa, of a liberal family among the founders of San José. His brother Miguel briefly held the presidency in 1849 and another brother, José Joaquín, became a general. Without a university education, Mora made his living in commerce, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane and real estate, and in 1842 formed a partnership with Vicente Aguilar that became one of the most powerful firms of the era. He was sworn in as the first Mayor of San José on 15 May 1837.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Elected vice president in 1847, he became acting ruler after a coup led by General José Manuel Quirós y Blanco forced President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-castro-madriz">José María Castro Madriz</a> to resign, and then won an election that has been described as farcical. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> recognised Costa Rican independence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a> created the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> on 1 March 1850. Mora set up a second barracks to check Quirós, who rose in arms on 3 June 1850 and was defeated. Facing a hostile Congress, he dissolved it and secured a loyal one, was re-elected in 1853 and improved the road from Cartago to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puntarenas">Puntarenas</a>.</p><p>In the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a>, Mora went with the troops in 1856, and the army won at Santa Rosa, Sardinal and Rivas before cholera broke out. Mora brought the troops home, and the disease then spread through much of the country, killing about 10,000 people, nearly a tenth of the population. In the second campaign Major Máximo Blanco, helped by the mariner Sylvanus Spencer, cut Walker's supply line on the San Juan River, ending the war in April 1857. A later quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> ended in the Cañas-Jerez Treaty, which recognised Nicaragua's ownership of the San Juan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Re-elected in 1859, he was overthrown on 14 August by Colonel Lorenzo Salazar and Major Blanco and shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. On 17 September 1860 he landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puntarenas">Puntarenas</a> with his brother José Joaquín and his brother-in-law General José María Cañas, but was defeated at the Battle of La Angostura. The British consul Richard Farrer, with whom he sought asylum, handed him over, and he was shot at Los Lobos on 30 September 1860; Cañas was executed two days later.</p><p>The journalist and historian Armando Vargas Araya holds that the Montealegre and Castro Madriz families paid for forgeries to justify the killing. The Legislative Assembly declared Mora a national hero and liberator on 16 September 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan Rafael Mora Porras” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The other Central American states, led by Costa Rica under <b>Juan Rafael Mora</b>, united against him in the National War: Nicaraguan forces defeated his men at the Battle of San Jacinto on 14 September 1856, and Walker surrendered at Rivas in May 1857.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Costa Rican militia under General <b>Juan Rafael Mora Porras</b> fought the Nicaraguan forces of the American mercenary William Walker.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-11">11 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Juan Rafael Mora Porras</b> led the Central American war that defeated William Walker in 1856 to 1857, in which the drummer Juan Santamaría became a national hero.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-costa-rica">Republic of Costa Rica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-costa-rica">San José</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>. Died in Puntarenas, in what is now <a href="/countries/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9.93,-84.08&amp;year=1843" aria-label="San José on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2231 1793 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juan Rafael Mora Porras"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2681 2063h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2681 2063h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=9.93,-84.08&amp;year=1843">What was here in 1843? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
